---
layout: app-shell.njk
permalink: /transit/
title: "Transit | Stefanos Tzortzoglou"
shell: true
bodyClass: "bg-brand-bg text-brand-text antialiased min-h-screen flex flex-col"
navDrawerOnly: true
signOut: true
bodyScripts:
  - src: "/scripts/transit.js"
    type: "module"
  - src: "/scripts/contact-widget.js"
    type: "module"
  - src: "/scripts/palette.js"
    type: "module"
---
<section class="app-page">

  {# The heading and the board switch are stacked rather than set against each other.

     Sharing a justify-between row, they fitted side by side until the operator badge arrived in
     the heading, at which point the switch dropped to its own line - 52px of movement caused by
     a 24px badge. Stacked, the switch is on its own line from the first paint and the badge can
     appear without moving anything.

     Only where the room is tight, though. From `sm` up the two sit against each other again the
     way they did: the badge has space to appear there without wrapping anything, so the stability
     costs nothing and the composition is worth keeping. #}
  <div class="mb-2 sm:flex sm:flex-wrap sm:items-center sm:justify-between sm:gap-4">
    <h1 class="flex items-center gap-3 text-3xl font-bold text-brand-text">
      {% icon "fa-solid-bus", "h-7 w-7 text-brand-accent" %}
      <span>Transit</span>
    </h1>

    {# Departures and arrivals are two boards for one stop, so they are one control with two
       states rather than two links. aria-pressed rather than a radio group: these are buttons
       that change what is below them, not a value being submitted anywhere. #}
    <div class="mt-3 sm:mt-0 inline-flex rounded-lg bg-brand-bg p-1" role="group" aria-label="Which board">
      <button type="button" id="trDep" class="seg" aria-pressed="true">Departures</button>
      <button type="button" id="trArr" class="seg" aria-pressed="false">Arrivals</button>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div id="status" role="status" aria-live="polite" class="mb-6 text-sm"></div>

  {# --- the stop ------------------------------------------------------------
     The heading is the stop, because every number on this page is about it. The controls sit
     beside it rather than in a toolbar of their own: there are two of them. #}
  <div class="flex flex-wrap items-end justify-between gap-x-4 gap-y-1 mb-1">
    <h2 id="board-heading" class="text-xl font-bold text-brand-text">&nbsp;</h2>
    {# The operator sits with the stop, not in the heading. It is a fact the board reports - it
       changes when you point this at a stop in Skane - so it belongs where the other facts about
       the board are. As a badge beside the title it had to reserve an empty box from first paint
       to avoid wrapping the heading when it arrived, which is the gap that box was. Here it
       arrives with the rest of the line and nothing moves. #}
    <div class="text-sm text-brand-muted sm:flex sm:items-end sm:gap-x-2">
      <p class="min-h-5"><span id="trOperator"></span><span id="trAge" class="tabular-nums"></span></p>
      <p class="-ml-1.5 sm:ml-0">
        <button type="button" id="trChange" class="link-action">Change stop</button>
        <button type="button" id="trRefresh" class="link-action ml-1">Refresh</button>
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    {# Closed until asked for, the same as the card on the home view. Two ways in: a name, or
       where you are standing. Neither is offered as the default, because the browser asks for
       permission before the second one answers and a page must not spend that on page load. #}
    <form id="trPicker" method="get" class="hidden mb-4 border-b border-brand-border pb-4">
      <label for="trSearch" class="field-label text-sm">Search for a stop</label>
      <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2">
        <input type="text" id="trSearch" name="trSearch" autocomplete="off" maxlength="80"
          placeholder="Portalgatan" class="field flex-1 min-w-[12rem]">
        <button type="submit" class="btn-primary text-sm">Search</button>
        <button type="button" id="trNear" class="btn-secondary text-sm">
          {% icon "fa-solid-location-dot", "h-3.5 w-3.5" %}<span class="ml-1.5">Near me</span>
        </button>
        <button type="button" id="trCancel" class="btn-secondary text-sm">Cancel</button>
      </div>
      <ul id="trResults" class="mt-3 space-y-1"></ul>
      <p id="trPickNote" role="status" aria-live="polite" class="hint mt-2"></p>
    </form>

    {# Twelve, because that is what the API returns at most and what a board normally shows.
       Reserving fewer would trade a large jump for a smaller one, which is not the point. Each
       placeholder is the two-line shape of a real row: line badge, destination over its
       scheduled time, and the countdown. renderBoard() clears the list. #}
    <ul id="trList" aria-busy="true" class="divide-y divide-brand-border">
      {%- for i in range(0, 12) %}
      <li class="board-row" aria-hidden="true">
        <span class="skel h-6 w-7"></span>
        <span class="min-w-0">
          <span class="skel block h-4 w-3/4"></span>
          <span class="skel mt-2 block h-3 w-1/2"></span>
        </span>
        <span class="skel h-4 w-12"></span>
      </li>
      {%- endfor %}
    </ul>
    <p id="trNote" class="text-sm text-brand-muted"></p>
  </div>

  {# --- what stops here -----------------------------------------------------
     Derived from the board that is already on screen, not fetched: every line on it, and where
     each one goes. ResRobot has no "lines serving this stop" call, so this is the honest
     version of that - what has actually turned up in the next hour, rather than a timetable
     this does not have. The heading says as much. #}
  <section aria-labelledby="lines-heading" class="mt-10">
    <h2 id="lines-heading" class="text-xl font-bold text-brand-text mb-1">Lines on this board</h2>
    {# The wording flips with the board: on arrivals these are the places buses are coming from,
       and a heading that says "headed" over a list of origins is worse than no heading. #}
    <p id="trLinesNote" class="text-sm text-brand-muted mb-4">
      Every line due in the next hour, and where each one is headed.
    </p>
    {# Two placeholders, matching the shape renderLines draws: a line badge beside the places
       that line goes. Two because a stop is usually served by two lines - this one is - and a
       list whose length depends on the timetable cannot be reserved exactly. Reserving the
       typical count is the whole of what is available here; see the note on the board above. #}
    <ul id="trLines" aria-busy="true" class="grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
      {%- for i in range(0, 2) %}
      <li class="card flex items-start gap-3 py-3" aria-hidden="true">
        <span class="skel dep-line mt-0.5 w-7"></span>
        <span class="skel h-8 min-w-0 flex-1"></span>
      </li>
      {%- endfor %}
    </ul>
  </section>

  {# --- around this stop ----------------------------------------------------
     A picture of where the stop is, of the stops around you once you have shared a location, and
     of where one departure goes next once you have chosen one from the board.

     The map is centred from the board's own coordinate, so it needs no permission - which is the
     whole reason this section is no longer hidden until one is granted. A map of the stop you are
     watching should not depend on being willing to say where you are standing. The nearby stops
     still do, because finding them needs a coordinate to search around.

     **What the line between two dots is, and what it is not.** The board now carries the stops
     each row calls at on the far side of this one - after it on a departures board, before it on
     an arrivals board - in order, so the sequence is real data. The shape of the
     leg between two of them is not: ResRobot gives no geometry, so what is drawn is a straight
     chord across whatever is in between. It is dashed and the copy below says so, because a solid
     line between two places on a map is read as a road, and this app does not claim a control it
     does not have - in a picture any more than in a sentence. "Lines on this board" above answers
     "where does this line go" without drawing anything, and stays exactly as it is. #}
  <section aria-labelledby="around-heading" class="mt-10">
    <h2 id="around-heading" class="text-xl font-bold text-brand-text mb-1">Around this stop</h2>
    {# Worded for both boards rather than for the departures one. The stops a row carries are the
       ones on the far side of this stop, and which side that is turns round with the board: ahead
       of the bus on departures, behind it on arrivals. The note under the map names the direction
       in full once a row has been pressed. #}
    <p class="text-sm text-brand-muted mb-4">
      Press any departure above and its route appears here. The dots are the stops it calls at;
      the dashes are the order they come in, not the roads the bus drives.
    </p>

    {# Hidden until there is a coordinate to centre it on and a library to draw it with; the note
       below says which of the two is missing. An explicit height because a map has no content of
       its own to be the height of, and `p-0` because a card's padding would leave the tiles
       floating inside a frame.

       Taller than it was, because it now has a route across it rather than one dot in the middle:
       at the old height a sequence of six stops fitted into a band a couple of centimetres deep
       and the basemap under it was too cramped to say which part of town any of them was in. #}
    <div id="trMap" class="card hidden h-80 overflow-hidden p-0 sm:h-[28rem]"></div>
    {# Two notes, because they are announced differently and that is the only reason.

       The first is not a live region, unlike the notes above it: it is rewritten on every board
       load, mostly with the sentence it already had, and three announcements a refresh is how a
       live region stops being read. It also carries showMap()'s reasons for there being no map.

       The second is, and has to be. It is written only when a row on the board is pressed, and
       what that press changes is a canvas - unreachable, and silent to a screen reader. Without a
       polite announcement here, pressing a row reorders a picture nobody is told about. #}
    <p id="trMapNote" class="hint"></p>
    <p id="trRouteNote" role="status" aria-live="polite" class="hint"></p>

    {# The list survives the map rather than being replaced by it: it carries the distance in
       metres, and "how far away is it" is a question a number answers better than a picture.
       Still revealed only once a location has been shared, for the reason it always was - an
       empty "Stops near you" on a page that has never been given one is a section that exists
       to be disappointed by. #}
    <div id="trNearby" class="hidden mt-6">
      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-brand-text mb-1">Stops near you</h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-brand-muted mb-4">
        Straight-line distance, as ResRobot measures it. Choosing one makes it your stop
        everywhere, including the card on the home view.
      </p>
      <ul id="trNearList" class="grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2"></ul>
    </div>
  </section>

</section>
